      <div>
        <h1 class="ForewordTitle">Foreword</h1>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="foreword" id="foreword"></a>ISO (the International Organization for Standardization)
is a worldwide federation of national standards bodies (ISO member bodies). The work of preparing International Standards is normally carried out through ISO technical committees. Each member body interested in a subject for which a technical committee has been established has the right to be represented on that committee. International organizations, governmental and non-governmental, in liaison with ISO, also take part in the work. ISO collaborates closely with the International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC) on all matters of electrotechnical standardization.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2c99a724-ae1f-412f-9081-7eeb9cf65512" id="_2c99a724-ae1f-412f-9081-7eeb9cf65512"></a>The procedures used to develop this document and those intended for its further maintenance are described in the ISO/IEC Directives, Part 1. In particular the different approval criteria needed for the different types of ISO documents should be noted. This document was drafted in accordance with the editorial rules of the ISO/IEC Directives, Part 2 (see www.iso.org/directives).</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_273e7075-3c26-482d-9ed5-3603e405eca6" id="_273e7075-3c26-482d-9ed5-3603e405eca6"></a>Attention is drawn to the possibility that some of the elements of this document may be the subject of patent rights. ISO shall not be held responsible for identifying any or all such patent rights. Details of any patent rights identified during the development of the document will be in the Introduction and/or on the ISO list of patent declarations received (see www.iso.org/patents).</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6360ae9d-1cc3-47d8-961b-d862ffaa3b02" id="_6360ae9d-1cc3-47d8-961b-d862ffaa3b02"></a>Any trade name used in this document is information given for the convenience of users and does not constitute an endorsement.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_597fddd7-6ab5-43e5-a184-8d9976695bd6" id="_597fddd7-6ab5-43e5-a184-8d9976695bd6"></a>For an explanation on the voluntary nature of standards, the meaning of ISO specific terms and expressions related to conformity assessment, as well as information about ISO&#x2019;s adherence to the World Trade Organization (WTO) principles in the Technical Barriers to Trade (TBT) see the following URL: www.iso.org/iso/foreword.html.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_250a1250-64ed-4d81-bb70-8677f077edaa" id="_250a1250-64ed-4d81-bb70-8677f077edaa"></a>This document was prepared by Technical Committee ISO/TC 37, <i>Language and terminology</i>, Subcommittee 4, <i>Language resource management</i>,
Work Group 4, <i>Lexical Resources</i>.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_03257ffe-0f02-48fb-a4cd-0b25906aab3e" id="_03257ffe-0f02-48fb-a4cd-0b25906aab3e"></a>This is the first edition of this document.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="MsoNormal">
        <br clear="all" style="mso-special-character:line-break;page-break-before:always"/>
      </p>
      <div class="Section3"><a name="introduction" id="introduction"></a>
        <h1 class="IntroTitle">Introduction</h1>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b00fb924-7fc6-477a-b01d-b233ca807282" id="_b00fb924-7fc6-477a-b01d-b233ca807282"></a>Personal names are among the most commonly exchanged information on the
Internet. However, the detail of personal names during interchange
often recedes to the lowest common denominator understood by
both sides, which is a plain string.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e0a5d75e-a368-464a-95a0-f8f429b28e31" id="_e0a5d75e-a368-464a-95a0-f8f429b28e31"></a>Such exchange invariably entails the loss of most semantic
structure of a personal name, causing a range off issues from
inappropriate greetings at hotels to failure of name verification at
national borders.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_454564c1-f33f-4f99-a5a9-30b75f491980" id="_454564c1-f33f-4f99-a5a9-30b75f491980"></a>This International Standard facilitates the interchange and interaction
of names between humans and applications without loss of fidelity.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a19a227e-a2d3-4990-989d-b033c0b9524b" id="_a19a227e-a2d3-4990-989d-b033c0b9524b"></a>This standard addresses the structure of personal names
in ISO standards covering topics such as bibliographic management,
personal identification documents, electronic messaging,
e-commerce to postal applications.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="MsoNormal">&#xA0;</p>
    </div>
    <p class="MsoNormal">
      <br clear="all" class="section"/>
    </p>
    <div class="WordSection3">
      <p class="zzSTDTitle1">Computer applications in terminology&#xA0;&#x2014; Naming&#xA0;&#x2014; Structure of personal names</p>
      <div><a name="_scope" id="_scope"></a>
        <h1>1<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Scope</h1>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_44d08302-d03b-4d2e-86dd-f8015e7d5bd3" id="_44d08302-d03b-4d2e-86dd-f8015e7d5bd3"></a>This document specifies a reference model for &#x201C;personal names&#x201D;,
suitable for machine encoding, for digital storage and transmission.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2949f0ae-02ef-4436-baa7-ebfb83cc062a" id="_2949f0ae-02ef-4436-baa7-ebfb83cc062a"></a>The reference model is composed of the following data elements:</p>
        
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo1;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    personal name components;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo1;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    cultural name profiles for personal name patterns; and
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo1;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    template definitions for the display and input of personal names.
  </p>

        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_27982d3a-bbaf-4ced-bbec-6cf134f56f74" id="_27982d3a-bbaf-4ced-bbec-6cf134f56f74"></a>This document does not handle the linkage of a personal name to an identity.</p>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_da7e0294-a437-4dca-9bb2-6a1ba81b70e0" id="_da7e0294-a437-4dca-9bb2-6a1ba81b70e0"></a>The personal name model is used to encode a person&#x2019;s personal names for:</p>
        
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo2;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    as a static data element
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo2;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    as an interoperable data structure used with databases
  </p>

        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d9f1a659-aafc-43fc-98a0-0e983dd171d3" id="_d9f1a659-aafc-43fc-98a0-0e983dd171d3"></a>The cultural name profile model is used to guide rendering of a
name model instance as a string
as appropriate to the applied cultural name profile.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h1>2<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Normative references</h1>
        <p class="MsoNormal">The following documents are referred to in the text in such a way that some or all of their content constitutes requirements of this document. For dated references, only the edition cited applies. For undated references, the latest edition of the referenced document (including any amendments) applies.</p>
        <p class="NormRef"><a name="ISO639-1" id="ISO639-1"></a>ISO 639-1, <i> Codes for the representation of names of languages</i></p>
        <p class="NormRef"><a name="ISO15924" id="ISO15924"></a>ISO 15924, <i> Codes for the representation of names of scripts</i></p>
        <p class="NormRef"><a name="ISO24229" id="ISO24229"></a>ISO 24229, <i> Information and documentation&#x2009;&#x2014;&#x2009;Codes for script conversion systems</i></p>
      </div>
      <div><a name="terms" id="terms"></a><h1>3<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Terms and definitions</h1><p class="MsoNormal">For the purposes of this document,
    the following terms and definitions apply.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">ISO and IEC maintain terminological databases for use in
standardization at the following addresses:</p>


<p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo3;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst"> ISO Online browsing platform: available at
  <a href="http://www.iso.org/obp">http://www.iso.org/obp</a> </p>
<p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo3;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast"> IEC Electropedia: available at
  <a href="http://www.electropedia.org">http://www.electropedia.org</a>
 </p> 
<div class="Note"><p class="Note">???: </p></div><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-name" id="term-name"></a>3.1</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">name</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d701e9d4-a9e3-4945-9d7e-6fc06697d529" id="_d701e9d4-a9e3-4945-9d7e-6fc06697d529"></a>verbal <i>designation</i> (<a href="#ISO5127">ISO 5127:2017, 3.1.5.24</a>) of an <i>individual concept</i> (<a href="#ISO5127">ISO 5127:2017, 3.1.2.16</a>)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO5127">ISO 5127:2017, 3.1.5.28</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-person" id="term-person"></a>3.2</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">person</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f683f12d-80d0-408a-aa13-9f15c072e1b2" id="_f683f12d-80d0-408a-aa13-9f15c072e1b2"></a>human being</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO29003">ISO/IEC TS 29003:2018, 3.12</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-personal-name" id="term-personal-name"></a>3.3</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">personal name</p>
  <p class="AltTerms" style="text-align:left;">anthroponym</p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2bf44464-ca17-43d7-9733-6e9a5a9739d2" id="_2bf44464-ca17-43d7-9733-6e9a5a9739d2"></a>name (<a href="#term-name">3.1</a>) of a <i>person</i> (<a href="#term-person">3.2</a>)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO5127">ISO 5127:2017, 3.1.5.43</a>, modified &mdash; preferred term <i>personal name</i> used in this document,
replaced term <i>human being</i> with term <i>person</i> used in this document.]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-locale" id="term-locale"></a>3.4</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">locale</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c196a5f7-20a1-467f-b3eb-e1fd35091441" id="_c196a5f7-20a1-467f-b3eb-e1fd35091441"></a>definition of the subset of a user&#x2019;s environment that depends on
language and cultural conventions</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO9945">ISO/IEC/IEEE 9945:2009, 4.211</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-criterion" id="term-criterion"></a>3.5</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">criterion</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b7ab2795-470c-498f-8c6b-36f6013b05b9" id="_b7ab2795-470c-498f-8c6b-36f6013b05b9"></a>threshold or value of a variable to be met</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO14198">ISO/TS 14198:2012, 3.2</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-name-display-profile" id="term-name-display-profile"></a>3.6</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">name display profile</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f276d2e9-821c-48ad-8c1a-54cb66227e54" id="_f276d2e9-821c-48ad-8c1a-54cb66227e54"></a>profile that associates a set of <i>criteria</i>
(<a href="#term-criterion">3.5</a>) and <i>layout templates</i> (<a href="#term-name-display-template">3.12</a>)</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="term-data-type" id="term-data-type"></a>3.7</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">data type</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_35c53b8f-b18f-4e66-82bd-375831625026" id="_35c53b8f-b18f-4e66-82bd-375831625026"></a>set of representable values</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO9075-1">ISO/IEC 9075-1:2016, 3.1.1.4</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-primitive-type" id="term-primitive-type"></a>3.8</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">primitive type</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_01b74428-aa40-48d6-9c30-a1a223ea03e3" id="_01b74428-aa40-48d6-9c30-a1a223ea03e3"></a>pre-defined basic datatype without any substructure, such as an integer or a
string</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO14813-5">ISO 14813-5:2010, B.1.118</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-localized-string" id="term-localized-string"></a>3.9</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">localized string</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_3a8be63f-e995-43dc-b174-b40d74830741" id="_3a8be63f-e995-43dc-b174-b40d74830741"></a>user-context-dependent string determined by a particular <i>locale</i> (<a href="#term-locale">3.4</a>)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO20944-1">ISO/IEC 20944-1:2013, 3.21.10.2</a>, modified &mdash; added reference to term <i>locale</i>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-template-fragment" id="term-template-fragment"></a>3.10</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">template fragment</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_741d12a3-1326-4012-9b2c-a1564b5aabd8" id="_741d12a3-1326-4012-9b2c-a1564b5aabd8"></a><i>localized string</i> (<a href="#term-localized-string">3.9</a>)
or <i>name component</i> (<a href="#term-name-component">3.11</a>)
used in a <i>name display template</i> (<a href="#term-name-display-template">3.12</a>)</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="term-name-component" id="term-name-component"></a>3.11</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">name component</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_11e22b49-cbc6-4aa4-b85f-8c8d5e7687ee" id="_11e22b49-cbc6-4aa4-b85f-8c8d5e7687ee"></a>component that forms part of a <i>personal name</i> (<a href="#term-personal-name">3.3</a>)</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="term-name-display-template" id="term-name-display-template"></a>3.12</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">name display template</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a6c31f17-7daa-4c5e-9579-ed04779c4062" id="_a6c31f17-7daa-4c5e-9579-ed04779c4062"></a>specification of ordering of
<i>template fragments</i> (<a href="#term-template-fragment">3.10</a>)
for a <i>name display profile</i> (<a href="#term-name-display-profile">3.6</a>)
of a <i>cultural name display profile</i> (<a href="#term-cultural-name-display-profile">3.13</a>)</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="term-cultural-name-display-profile" id="term-cultural-name-display-profile"></a>3.13</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">cultural name display profile</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_8b6801a0-ff9a-4dda-a067-ea4dc3bd4da6" id="_8b6801a0-ff9a-4dda-a067-ea4dc3bd4da6"></a>set of <i>name display profiles</i> (<a href="#term-name-display-profile">3.6</a>)
pertaining to a particular <i>culture</i> (<a href="#term-culture">3.14</a>)</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="term-culture" id="term-culture"></a>3.14</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">culture</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a1db6bf8-592e-45e1-a4dd-cd487678b7cb" id="_a1db6bf8-592e-45e1-a4dd-cd487678b7cb"></a>pattern of beliefs and expectations shared by the organization&#x2019;s members</p>

<div class="Note"><p class="Note">Note 1 to entry: </p></div><p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO14105">ISO/TR 14105:2011, 2.1</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-language-id" id="term-language-id"></a>3.15</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">language identifier</p>
  <p class="AltTerms" style="text-align:left;">language symbol</p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_019f2440-b6fa-46ca-9168-6bb478180c07" id="_019f2440-b6fa-46ca-9168-6bb478180c07"></a>symbol that uniquely identifies a particular language</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO639-3">ISO 639-3, 3.3</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-script" id="term-script"></a>3.16</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">script</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2721e54b-62ed-4121-963b-c28d9c9c378f" id="_2721e54b-62ed-4121-963b-c28d9c9c378f"></a>set of graphic characters used for the written form of one or more languages</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO15924">ISO 15924, 3.7</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-script-code" id="term-script-code"></a>3.17</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">script code</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_1fbf3bff-a958-4450-9e02-9619b989bd1b" id="_1fbf3bff-a958-4450-9e02-9619b989bd1b"></a>combination of characters used to represent the name of a <i>script</i>
(<a href="#term-script">3.16</a>)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO15924">ISO 15924, 3.8</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="term-uri" id="term-uri"></a>3.18</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">URI</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_443712de-3d9a-4109-bdbc-d1bbd9825f59" id="_443712de-3d9a-4109-bdbc-d1bbd9825f59"></a>compact sequence of characters that identifies an abstract or physical resource</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">[SOURCE: <a href="#ISO12234-3">ISO 12234-3:2016, 3.4</a>]</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="_family_name" id="_family_name"></a>3.19</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">family name</p><p class="AltTerms" style="text-align:left;">surname</p>
<p class="AltTerms" style="text-align:left;">last name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_96bf1aa3-2005-4066-863b-8c9c1c3306f8" id="_96bf1aa3-2005-4066-863b-8c9c1c3306f8"></a>portion of a personal name that indicates a person&#x2019;s family, tribe or community, depending on the culture where the personal name originates from</p>
<div class="Note"><p class="Note">Note 1 to entry: Not all cultures provide a family name in a personal name.</p></div><p class="TermNum"><a name="_given_name" id="_given_name"></a>3.20</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">given name</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4ec1d0bf-594f-4fb9-923b-fc7d81820d40" id="_4ec1d0bf-594f-4fb9-923b-fc7d81820d40"></a>component of a personal name that can be used to identify a person</p>
<div class="Note"><p class="Note">Note 1 to entry: There could be more than one given name in a personal name.</p></div><p class="TermNum"><a name="_first_name" id="_first_name"></a>3.21</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">first name</p>
  <p class="AltTerms" style="text-align:left;">forename</p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6de7a8e9-23e1-47b3-a9e5-cedf3a248bde" id="_6de7a8e9-23e1-47b3-a9e5-cedf3a248bde"></a>given name used to identify a person in some cultures</p>
<div class="Note"><p class="Note">Note 1 to entry: There could be more than one first name in some cultures.</p></div><p class="TermNum"><a name="_middle_name" id="_middle_name"></a>3.22</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">middle name</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a5b0a9ba-6dca-42fb-a773-95748157efff" id="_a5b0a9ba-6dca-42fb-a773-95748157efff"></a>given name in some cultures that is not a first name or a family name</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="_genonymic" id="_genonymic"></a>3.23</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">genonymic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_3bdfe455-d647-47c4-93cf-be394e70fb4d" id="_3bdfe455-d647-47c4-93cf-be394e70fb4d"></a>name derived from given name of an ancestor</p>
<div class="Note"><p class="Note">Note 1 to entry: the genonymic could come from a parent, grandparent or an earlier ancestor.</p></div><p class="TermNum"><a name="_patronymic" id="_patronymic"></a>3.24</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">patronymic</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_84a3414e-9b9f-42e7-b207-d5d4d4496328" id="_84a3414e-9b9f-42e7-b207-d5d4d4496328"></a>genonymic derived from given name of father</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="_matronymic" id="_matronymic"></a>3.25</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">matronymic</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d67abc1c-09eb-41c1-a02d-c644505a7767" id="_d67abc1c-09eb-41c1-a02d-c644505a7767"></a>genonymic derived from given name of mother</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="_avonymic" id="_avonymic"></a>3.26</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">avonymic</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_606a5ee0-2ebe-46b2-ba1f-07c5673e08c0" id="_606a5ee0-2ebe-46b2-ba1f-07c5673e08c0"></a>genonymic derived from given name of grandfather</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="_paedonymic" id="_paedonymic"></a>3.27</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">paedonymic</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_afd4c696-2641-4e21-8e58-a580dce92f70" id="_afd4c696-2641-4e21-8e58-a580dce92f70"></a>given name derived from given name of child</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="_mononym" id="_mononym"></a>3.28</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">mononym</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_424355bc-847b-4a43-84c2-9d884ebcd9c9" id="_424355bc-847b-4a43-84c2-9d884ebcd9c9"></a>given name that is the entirety of a personal name</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="_style" id="_style"></a>3.29</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">style</p><p class="AltTerms" style="text-align:left;">honorific</p>
<p class="AltTerms" style="text-align:left;">honorific title</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a21ac2e9-7c6f-49a7-a322-0b7a47329a52" id="_a21ac2e9-7c6f-49a7-a322-0b7a47329a52"></a>name or title given to, or used by, a person as a mark of status or respect</p><p class="TermNum"><a name="_religious_name" id="_religious_name"></a>3.30</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">religious name</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4547ee5f-5d46-4276-9604-6a930ada9d45" id="_4547ee5f-5d46-4276-9604-6a930ada9d45"></a>name or title given to, or used by, a person according to the tradition of the person&#x2019;s religion</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="_title" id="_title"></a>3.31</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">title</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ab02c97b-0c1e-42ce-9f2f-d77e60dcdcdc" id="_ab02c97b-0c1e-42ce-9f2f-d77e60dcdcdc"></a>name that describes a position or job</p>
<p class="TermNum"><a name="_nickname" id="_nickname"></a>3.32</p><p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">nickname</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_1d5b4694-0dcc-4a98-baba-4b7e45de07c7" id="_1d5b4694-0dcc-4a98-baba-4b7e45de07c7"></a>familiar name given to a person instead of or as well as the real personal name</p>

<div class="example"><a name="_a2633b1b-2be8-4847-9e45-c3b7b8316531" id="_a2633b1b-2be8-4847-9e45-c3b7b8316531"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE 1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Misha&#x201D; for &#x201C;Mikhail&#x201D; in Russian</p></div><div class="example"><a name="_f57753ea-26f3-4073-a7ca-d011f263faa9" id="_f57753ea-26f3-4073-a7ca-d011f263faa9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE 2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Bill&#x201D; for &#x201C;William&#x201D; in English</p></div><p class="TermNum"><a name="_maiden_name" id="_maiden_name"></a>3.33</p>
  <p class="Terms" style="text-align:left;">maiden name</p>
  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_aef6af66-c91c-4d88-9276-5fb6d8e310eb" id="_aef6af66-c91c-4d88-9276-5fb6d8e310eb"></a>family name that a married woman used prior to its being changed at marriage</p>
</div>
      <div><a name="data-types" id="data-types"></a>
        <h1>4<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Data types</h1>
        <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c2aea7d0-57d2-440a-ab87-29fa3b7cc9ce" id="_c2aea7d0-57d2-440a-ab87-29fa3b7cc9ce"></a>These are the basic data types used in this document.</p>
        <div class="figure"><a name="_22561739-029d-4d5b-8d44-3091b9f279a8" id="_22561739-029d-4d5b-8d44-3091b9f279a8"></a>
  <img src="iso-37100_files/669f9082-e1b9-4f92-8be6-55e593c58dfa.png" width="400" height="46"/>
<p class="FigureTitle" align="center">Figure 1</p></div>
        <table class="dl">
          <tr>
            <td valign="top" align="left">
              <p align="left" style="margin-left:0pt;text-align:left;" class="MsoNormal">Uri</p>
            </td>
            <td valign="top">
    <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_617c610c-4601-4798-a8f6-824e6cdb4762" id="_617c610c-4601-4798-a8f6-824e6cdb4762"></a>Container for URIs, as defined by <a href="#rfc3986">RFC 3986</a>.</p>
  </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td valign="top" align="left">
              <p align="left" style="margin-left:0pt;text-align:left;" class="MsoNormal">Iso639Code</p>
            </td>
            <td valign="top">
    <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_5a382c43-c765-4f50-9356-59f7ba03978c" id="_5a382c43-c765-4f50-9356-59f7ba03978c"></a>Code for language or language family, as specified in <a href="#iso639">ISO 639</a>.
The specification is open as to whether the two-letter code or the three-letter
code for languages is used.</p>
  </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td valign="top" align="left">
              <p align="left" style="margin-left:0pt;text-align:left;" class="MsoNormal">Iso15924Code</p>
            </td>
            <td valign="top">
    <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b29e71b0-0a95-4ed3-b8d2-369e82b13045" id="_b29e71b0-0a95-4ed3-b8d2-369e82b13045"></a>Code for scripts (writing scripts), as specified in <a href="#iso15924">ISO 15924:2004</a>.</p>
  </td>
          </tr>
        </table><p style="margin-top:0cm;margin-right:0cm;margin-bottom:0cm;margin-left:0.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:1.0pt;mso-line-height-rule:exactly" class="MsoNormal"><span lang="EN-GB" style="display:none;mso-hide:all" xml:lang="EN-GB">&#xA0;</span></p>
        <table class="dl">
          <tr>
            <td valign="top" align="left">
              <p align="left" style="margin-left:0pt;text-align:left;" class="MsoNormal">NameString</p>
            </td>
            <td valign="top">
    <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_740e4059-6778-42fc-b6ac-14b613fadf80" id="_740e4059-6778-42fc-b6ac-14b613fadf80"></a>String which optionally specifies its language and/or script.</p>
  </td>
          </tr>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div><a name="_personal_name" id="_personal_name"></a>
        <h1>5<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Personal name</h1>
        <div><a name="_general" id="_general"></a><h2>5.1 General</h2><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_22bbf5d6-2a62-49a5-8d09-850b07aa0994" id="_22bbf5d6-2a62-49a5-8d09-850b07aa0994"></a>A personal name is composed of multiple components.
Personal names are modelled with the cultural origin and information provided within a personal name in mind.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a5c5bd02-d929-4662-8568-ed526c6ca42c" id="_a5c5bd02-d929-4662-8568-ed526c6ca42c"></a>A personal name is represented by the following model.</p>
<div class="figure"><a name="_e00e7e1a-1e7f-4059-a206-c876ed9629ec" id="_e00e7e1a-1e7f-4059-a206-c876ed9629ec"></a>
  <img src="iso-37100_files/523787ce-2dcf-4693-ada9-45796d802739.png" width="400" height="368"/>
<p class="FigureTitle" align="center">Figure 2</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_modelling" id="_modelling"></a><h2>5.2 Modelling</h2><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4463ca10-a6c9-422b-b5cf-7a200fb23d19" id="_4463ca10-a6c9-422b-b5cf-7a200fb23d19"></a>Names are modelled in constituent components
or as a complete name string.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e7fa3540-c22b-4869-b16e-7cf5827def41" id="_e7fa3540-c22b-4869-b16e-7cf5827def41"></a>The <tt>initials</tt> component is used if all forenames are replaced by initials.
If only the middle name is replaced by an initial, it is still treated as a
forename.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_pseudonyms_and_variant_names" id="_pseudonyms_and_variant_names"></a><h2>5.3 Pseudonyms and variant names</h2>

  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2305e13b-bb8e-429c-a1ec-469885d94358" id="_2305e13b-bb8e-429c-a1ec-469885d94358"></a>Variant names, and assumed names, can both be modelled
as names.</p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_anonymous" id="_anonymous"></a><h2>5.4 Anonymous</h2>

  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_47713404-7b9e-4d3e-a855-b4c87342f075" id="_47713404-7b9e-4d3e-a855-b4c87342f075"></a>A representation of an intentionally anonymous personal name.</p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_unknown" id="_unknown"></a><h2>5.5 Unknown</h2>

  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_31e2c81a-60c6-4939-816f-614275ebd291" id="_31e2c81a-60c6-4939-816f-614275ebd291"></a>A representation of an unknown personal name.</p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_varii" id="_varii"></a><h2>5.6 Varii</h2><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_97361c68-0302-43b6-bb91-d06ef6a77604" id="_97361c68-0302-43b6-bb91-d06ef6a77604"></a>Descriptors of individuals, such as &#x201C;Various Authors&#x201D;, should be given as completeName
elements.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_46223b60-519f-47a9-8455-090bae5405bb" id="_46223b60-519f-47a9-8455-090bae5405bb"></a>As with pseudonyms, the real name of the creator if known can be
modelled as a note, or as a name variant.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div><a name="_name_component" id="_name_component"></a>
        <h1>6<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Name component</h1>
        <div><a name="_general_2" id="_general_2"></a><h2>6.1 General</h2>

  <div class="figure"><a name="_067013dd-57f9-44ca-93e4-1d4cc6a708f8" id="_067013dd-57f9-44ca-93e4-1d4cc6a708f8"></a>
  <img src="iso-37100_files/5e5e041e-5d2e-495f-8bf8-99fa5d7caca0.png" width="400" height="118"/>
<p class="FigureTitle" align="center">Figure 3</p></div>
</div>
        <div><a name="_pseudonym" id="_pseudonym"></a><h2>6.2 Pseudonym</h2><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7cd945ea-7bbf-4d74-ab73-ffde800b833d" id="_7cd945ea-7bbf-4d74-ab73-ffde800b833d"></a>A pseudonym or alias is a name that a person or group assumes for a particular
purpose, which can differ from their given name(s).</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo4;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Art name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo4;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Stage name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo4;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Nickname
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo4;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Username
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo4;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Pen name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo4;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Alias
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo4;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Code name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo4;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Regnal name
  </p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_family_name_2" id="_family_name_2"></a><h2>6.3 Family name</h2><div><a name="_general_3" id="_general_3"></a><h3>6.3.1 General</h3><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b36f928f-2ac4-4196-8199-072bcaee76f9" id="_b36f928f-2ac4-4196-8199-072bcaee76f9"></a>The family name is the portion of a personal name that indicates a person&#x2019;s family
(or tribe or community, depending on the culture).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_9ee722dc-f552-48f8-8e65-e7e89fb5b91a" id="_9ee722dc-f552-48f8-8e65-e7e89fb5b91a"></a>Depending on the culture, all members of a family unit may have identical family names or there may be variations based on the cultural rules.</p>
<div class="Note"><a name="_52edbcda-8093-4633-9e2c-9c4054d1cffd" id="_52edbcda-8093-4633-9e2c-9c4054d1cffd"></a><p class="Note"><span class="note_label">NOTE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Not all cultures provide a family name in a personal name.</p></div></div>
<div><a name="_compound_family_name" id="_compound_family_name"></a><h3>6.3.2 Compound family name</h3>

  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c8b98ad9-d38a-4b88-8b71-5192c5e84e47" id="_c8b98ad9-d38a-4b88-8b71-5192c5e84e47"></a>A compound family name is one based on merged family names.</p>
</div>
<div><a name="_icelandic_name" id="_icelandic_name"></a><h3>6.3.3 Icelandic name</h3>

</div>
<div><a name="_patrilineal_family_name" id="_patrilineal_family_name"></a><h3>6.3.4 Patrilineal family name</h3>

  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_785de1e5-ac86-46d8-a11b-7699b79b10ff" id="_785de1e5-ac86-46d8-a11b-7699b79b10ff"></a>A patrilineal family name is a name originated from the family name of the father.</p>
</div>
<div><a name="_matrilineal_family_name" id="_matrilineal_family_name"></a><h3>6.3.5 Matrilineal family name</h3>

  <p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_edef90d6-4113-4a14-808f-338d5bc2d8aa" id="_edef90d6-4113-4a14-808f-338d5bc2d8aa"></a>A matrilineal family name is a name originated from the family name of the mother.</p>
</div>
<div><a name="_matronymic_2" id="_matronymic_2"></a><h3>6.3.6 Matronymic</h3><p class="Sourcecode"><a name="_2e07b935-faab-4b2b-a7bc-5d032a73ade7" id="_2e07b935-faab-4b2b-a7bc-5d032a73ade7"></a>&lt;family-name&gt;<br/>&#xA0; &lt;type&gt;matronymic&lt;/type&gt;<br/>&#xA0; &lt;value&gt;Helguson&lt;/value&gt;<br/>&lt;/family-name&gt;</p>
</div>
<div><a name="_patronymic_2" id="_patronymic_2"></a><h3>6.3.7 Patronymic</h3><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_65ccbf98-8e1a-423c-a910-aa85e7d90951" id="_65ccbf98-8e1a-423c-a910-aa85e7d90951"></a>A patronymic name is a name originated from the given name of the father or a patrilineal ancestor.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_0c614f5b-e5d7-457d-99bf-a9531a020855" id="_0c614f5b-e5d7-457d-99bf-a9531a020855"></a>Different cultures have different ways of producing patronymic family names.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_f99c8581-3cf0-491d-a85c-99495c17bd78" id="_f99c8581-3cf0-491d-a85c-99495c17bd78"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Mongolian: Otryadyn G&#xFC;ndegmaa, O. G&#xFC;ndegmaa, Otryadyn is the patronymic family name</p></div></div>
<div><a name="_maiden_name_2" id="_maiden_name_2"></a><h3>6.3.8 Maiden name</h3>

</div>
<div><a name="_others" id="_others"></a><h3>6.3.9 Others</h3>

</div>
<div><a name="_mononymous" id="_mononymous"></a><h3>6.3.10 Mononymous</h3><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b5f9b7d8-97f8-4d55-aa12-e42348435dad" id="_b5f9b7d8-97f8-4d55-aa12-e42348435dad"></a>Some personal names are known and addressed by a single
given name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a7eb4582-15bd-432d-941d-e39b0db1e0c6" id="_a7eb4582-15bd-432d-941d-e39b0db1e0c6"></a>In some areas of the world, many people are known by a single
name, and so are said to be mononymous.<div class="Note"><a name="_831c3190-3134-4f24-bc69-ef52ab78959e" id="_831c3190-3134-4f24-bc69-ef52ab78959e"></a><p class="Note"><span class="note_label">NOTE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Mononymic name is considered as a given name without a family name.</p></div></p>

<div class="example"><a name="_f34e5858-2cca-4a3d-9288-d02d100e9309" id="_f34e5858-2cca-4a3d-9288-d02d100e9309"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Thant, third Secretary-General of the United Nations.</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_given_name_2" id="_given_name_2"></a><h2>6.4 Given name</h2><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_9812e09c-7f01-48ff-aa37-fd90f0307ee9" id="_9812e09c-7f01-48ff-aa37-fd90f0307ee9"></a>Diagram of naming conventions, using John Fitzgerald Kennedy as an example.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_75906146-42ff-44b8-acd7-111f4c8ce993" id="_75906146-42ff-44b8-acd7-111f4c8ce993"></a>The sarcophagus of Queen Desideria at Riddarholm Church in Sweden. The name was
given to Desideria Clary not at birth but when she was created Crown
Princess of Sweden in 1810.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d83f770e-13d3-4f97-8dc4-8347c16eee69" id="_d83f770e-13d3-4f97-8dc4-8347c16eee69"></a>A given name is usually is given to a person, normally to a child by
his or her parents at or close to the time of birth.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_bc9a8b9d-be67-408b-8fca-261e30b807c4" id="_bc9a8b9d-be67-408b-8fca-261e30b807c4"></a>By contrast, a family name (also known as a family name, last name, or gentile
name), which is normally inherited, is typically shared with other members of
one&#x2019;s immediate family. Regnal names and religious or monastic names are
special given names bestowed upon someone receiving a crown or entering a
religious order. Such a person then typically becomes known chiefly by that
name.</p>
<div><a name="_arabic_name" id="_arabic_name"></a><h3>6.4.1 Arabic name</h3><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_9ef471b8-5c99-432b-a826-1261544cf9f7" id="_9ef471b8-5c99-432b-a826-1261544cf9f7"></a>Arabic names were historically based on a long naming system; most
Arabs did not have given/middle/family names, but a full chain of
names. This system was mainly in use throughout the Arab world.</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo5;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Ism, given name, first name or personal name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo5;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Laqab, family name, nickname, title, honorific
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo5;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Nasab, patronymic series
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo5;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Nisbah, family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo5;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Kunya, teknonym (name of first son or daughter) or nickname
  </p>
</div>
<div><a name="_religious_name_2" id="_religious_name_2"></a><h3>6.4.2 Religious name</h3><div><a name="_christian_name_baptismal_name_confirmation_name" id="_christian_name_baptismal_name_confirmation_name"></a><h4>6.4.2.1 Christian name, baptismal name, confirmation name</h4>

  <div class="example"><a name="_e6561b74-2566-4b39-95b9-edd0c27dd733" id="_e6561b74-2566-4b39-95b9-edd0c27dd733"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The baptised name of &#x201C;Sun Yat Sun&#x201D;, the father of modern China, is &#x201C;Yat Sun&#x201D; (&#x65E5;&#x65B0;).</p></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_papal_name" id="_papal_name"></a><h4>6.4.2.2 Papal name</h4><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6c607f29-b550-46ed-8ca3-680812b53156" id="_6c607f29-b550-46ed-8ca3-680812b53156"></a>A papal name or pontificial name is the regnal name taken by a pope.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e639b4c7-ae59-4d82-b195-e449ee30e571" id="_e639b4c7-ae59-4d82-b195-e449ee30e571"></a>Both the head of the Catholic Church, usually known as the Pope, and the Pope of the Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria (Coptic Pope) choose papal names.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_3e9d72a1-a3c1-493c-abde-f91eb598042e" id="_3e9d72a1-a3c1-493c-abde-f91eb598042e"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>John Paul II.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_95b63e18-5269-44f4-ada0-6c2b7cf0cb82" id="_95b63e18-5269-44f4-ada0-6c2b7cf0cb82"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Tawadros II</p></div></div></div>
<div><a name="_generational_name" id="_generational_name"></a><h3>6.4.3 Generational name</h3><div class="example"><a name="_99ba485c-32b2-4c9f-805c-cf1fd8fb94ad" id="_99ba485c-32b2-4c9f-805c-cf1fd8fb94ad"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Jose Maria Rodrigues Jr.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_d78bd3b4-9f65-41e8-93c4-b82123f840a6" id="_d78bd3b4-9f65-41e8-93c4-b82123f840a6"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>King George VI</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_nicknames" id="_nicknames"></a><h2>6.5 Nicknames</h2><div class="example"><a name="_88366d66-42b6-4ed3-93a4-cc72e8555848" id="_88366d66-42b6-4ed3-93a4-cc72e8555848"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The given name &#x201C;Jos&#xE9;&#x201D; in Spanish often goes by the nickname &#x201C;Pepe&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_1da1a0fc-232a-4b3c-86cd-2e7ebae41a7f" id="_1da1a0fc-232a-4b3c-86cd-2e7ebae41a7f"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The given name &#x201C;William&#x201D; in English often goes by the nickname &#x201C;Bill&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_honorific" id="_honorific"></a><h2>6.6 Honorific</h2><div><a name="_general_4" id="_general_4"></a><h3>6.6.1 General</h3><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_094f5177-448a-433d-9acd-cfbed1efc1b5" id="_094f5177-448a-433d-9acd-cfbed1efc1b5"></a>A style of office or honorific is an official or legally recognized
title.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_df44a9a2-6cb1-421f-a741-32a9df3772fc" id="_df44a9a2-6cb1-421f-a741-32a9df3772fc"></a>An honorific is a title that conveys esteem or respect for position or rank
when used in addressing or referring to a person.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b5c74a53-d8f8-4d50-be8d-fb66b5601c0f" id="_b5c74a53-d8f8-4d50-be8d-fb66b5601c0f"></a>Different types of honorific:</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_972bc524-62b4-477d-903f-35ff17072de2" id="_972bc524-62b4-477d-903f-35ff17072de2"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Mr.&#x201D;, &#x201C;Ms.&#x201D;</p></div>
<div class="Note"><a name="_c4ae660e-2d1c-47fb-b4f4-bce868c56c65" id="_c4ae660e-2d1c-47fb-b4f4-bce868c56c65"></a><p class="Note"><span class="note_label">NOTE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Bibliographic entries often encounter this problem of separation of name and honorifics.</p></div></div>
<div><a name="_social" id="_social"></a><h3>6.6.2 Social</h3>

</div>
<div><a name="_professional" id="_professional"></a><h3>6.6.3 Professional</h3>

  <div class="example"><a name="_4932d602-7232-4acc-a6e3-b44b5a63885d" id="_4932d602-7232-4acc-a6e3-b44b5a63885d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Doctor, Dr.</p></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_honors" id="_honors"></a><h3>6.6.4 Honors</h3>

</div>
<div><a name="_academic" id="_academic"></a><h3>6.6.5 Academic</h3>

</div>
<div><a name="_royal" id="_royal"></a><h3>6.6.6 Royal</h3>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo6;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Her Majesty: style
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo6;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Queen: title
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo6;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Elizabeth: given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo6;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    The Queen Mother: nick name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_58e6d0c1-6f9e-4461-9124-ab479e5c9018" id="_58e6d0c1-6f9e-4461-9124-ab479e5c9018"></a>Titles and styles</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo7;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    4 August 1900 &#x2013; 16 February 1904: The Honourable Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo7;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    16 February 1904 &#x2013; 26 April 1923: Lady Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo7;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    26 April 1923 &#x2013; 11 December 1936: Her Royal Highness The Duchess of York
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo7;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    11 December 1936 &#x2013; 6 February 1952: Her Majesty The Queen
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo7;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    11 December 1936 &#x2013; 14 August 1947 (for British India): Her Imperial Majesty The Queen-Empress
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo7;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    6 February 1952 &#x2013; 30 March 2002: Her Majesty Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother
  </p>

<div class="example"><a name="_39c23c55-d5ee-4c59-bfdf-b27e29f0bb70" id="_39c23c55-d5ee-4c59-bfdf-b27e29f0bb70"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span></p>
  
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo8;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Duchess: rank
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo8;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    of: rank association word
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo8;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    York: rank association place name
  </p>

</div>
<div class="example"><a name="_518ed96c-5cff-454e-9f79-70466d4ac13e" id="_518ed96c-5cff-454e-9f79-70466d4ac13e"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span></p><p class="Sourcecode"><a name="_fa790521-81c8-42c5-9770-1d410fecfd42" id="_fa790521-81c8-42c5-9770-1d410fecfd42"></a>&lt;style&gt;urn:iso:name:style:uk:hrh&lt;/style&gt;<br/>&lt;honorific&gt;<br/>&#xA0; &lt;rank&gt;<br/>&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0; &lt;type&gt;urn:iso:name:honorific:rank:duchess&lt;/type&gt;<br/>&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0; &lt;place&gt;York&lt;/place&gt;<br/>&#xA0; &lt;/rank&gt;<br/>&lt;/honorific&gt;</p>

<p class="Sourcecode"><a name="_b3f2607c-926e-4739-bf6e-6c9c4190dcc1" id="_b3f2607c-926e-4739-bf6e-6c9c4190dcc1"></a>&lt;urn:rank&gt;<br/>Duchess<br/>&#xA0; &lt;urn:rank:place&gt;<br/>&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0; &lt;urn:rank:place:association&gt;<br/>&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0; of<br/>&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0; &lt;/urn:rank:place:association&gt;<br/>&#xA0; York<br/>&#xA0; &lt;/urn:rank:place&gt;<br/>&lt;/urn:rank&gt;</p>
</div></div>
<div><a name="_style_of_office" id="_style_of_office"></a><h3>6.6.7 Style of office</h3><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_27acc8d6-fa1c-48ef-9729-7c6d70870c68" id="_27acc8d6-fa1c-48ef-9729-7c6d70870c68"></a>A style of office is an official or legally recognized
title.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7574e67a-2ebc-4966-9f53-f82ab3f115bc" id="_7574e67a-2ebc-4966-9f53-f82ab3f115bc"></a>Such styles are associated with
monarchies, presidents, members of legislative bodies, judges,
office holders and religious figures.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_97833b54-14c7-42fc-a46a-331b1f1a9a95" id="_97833b54-14c7-42fc-a46a-331b1f1a9a95"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Avul Pakir Jainulabdeen Abdul Kalam&#x201D;, former President of India, styled as &#x201C;His Excellency Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_3dfa6cc5-b367-45a1-b8cf-7276fb8e4345" id="_3dfa6cc5-b367-45a1-b8cf-7276fb8e4345"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;The Honorable Ruth Bader Ginsburg&#x201D; in the United States.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_ffc73bcf-4dd2-42e8-9bc9-ac557007e48b" id="_ffc73bcf-4dd2-42e8-9bc9-ac557007e48b"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;His Honour Judge Michael Wong&#x201D; in Hong Kong.</p></div></div>
<div><a name="_rank" id="_rank"></a><h3>6.6.8 Rank</h3>

  <div class="example"><a name="_0afbb010-0cd9-4522-badd-d6ce2a31f972" id="_0afbb010-0cd9-4522-badd-d6ce2a31f972"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Esquire as a social rank in UK (lawyer in US)</p></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_position" id="_position"></a><h3>6.6.9 Position</h3>

</div>
<div><a name="_qualification" id="_qualification"></a><h3>6.6.10 Qualification</h3>

  
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo9;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    &#x201C;Dr.&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo9;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;J.D.&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo9;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;M.D.&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo9;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;Ph.D.&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo9;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    &#x201C;Ir.&#x201D;
  </p>

</div>
<div><a name="_occupation" id="_occupation"></a><h3>6.6.11 Occupation</h3>

  
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    &#x201C;Doctor&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;Esquire&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;Captain&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;Coach&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;Officer&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;Reverend&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;Father&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    &#x201C;Rabbi&#x201D;
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo10;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    &#x201C;Professor&#x201D;
  </p>

</div>
<div><a name="_role" id="_role"></a><h3>6.6.12 Role</h3>

  <div class="example"><a name="_b5df2636-a326-4ce8-a0d1-bc7511a40aad" id="_b5df2636-a326-4ce8-a0d1-bc7511a40aad"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Ex-President</p></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_honors_2" id="_honors_2"></a><h3>6.6.13 Honors</h3><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_895bd31a-a20a-430f-93aa-2392aafd3a48" id="_895bd31a-a20a-430f-93aa-2392aafd3a48"></a>Orders of chivalry confer titles to its holders.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_c2b74525-f254-491a-998e-264439eb7afb" id="_c2b74525-f254-491a-998e-264439eb7afb"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The Most Excellent Order of the British Empire is a British order of chivalry, conferring appointments to classes including: &#x201C;Knight Grand Cross or Dame Grand Cross of the Most Excellent Order of the British Empire (GBE)&#x201D;,
&#x201C;Knight Commander or Dame Commander of the Most Excellent Order of the British Empire (KBE or DBE)&#x201D;,
&#x201C;Commander of the Most Excellent Order of the British Empire (CBE)&#x201D;,
&#x201C;Officer of the Most Excellent Order of the British Empire (OBE)&#x201D; and
&#x201C;Member of the Most Excellent Order of the British Empire (MBE)&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_e31c285c-805c-4f8c-82c3-e4e6c279d531" id="_e31c285c-805c-4f8c-82c3-e4e6c279d531"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Sir Hugh Beach, GBE KCB MC&#x201D;.</p></div></div></div>
      </div>
      <div><a name="_name_pattern" id="_name_pattern"></a>
        <h1>7<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Name pattern</h1>
        <div><a name="_general_5" id="_general_5"></a><h2>7.1 General</h2><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7508614e-2d13-4d8a-8497-27fcb9295799" id="_7508614e-2d13-4d8a-8497-27fcb9295799"></a>Personal name order depends on the cultural profile of the name.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_f7b09542-f2e1-40b5-857a-ce02976e842f" id="_f7b09542-f2e1-40b5-857a-ce02976e842f"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The name order of &#x201C;[given name(s)] [family name]&#x201D; is commonly used
throughout most European countries.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_9b344e13-f1dd-4485-bb7a-cfdcdbd493bf" id="_9b344e13-f1dd-4485-bb7a-cfdcdbd493bf"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The name order of &#x201C;[family name] [given name(s)]&#x201D; is commonly used
in East Asia (for example in China, Japan, Korea, Malaysian
Chinese, Singapore, and Vietnam, among others), as well as in Southern and
North-Eastern parts of India, and in Hungary.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_c1f7645d-a4cd-49e1-945b-03d7237e3048" id="_c1f7645d-a4cd-49e1-945b-03d7237e3048"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The name order of &#x201C;[given name(s)] [father&#x2019;s family name] [mother&#x2019;s family name]&#x201D;
is commonly used in Spanish-speaking countries such as Spain and Uruguay.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_abbreviation" id="_abbreviation"></a><h2>7.2 Abbreviation</h2>

  <div class="example"><a name="_834a90d4-d27a-480d-9295-030744bb4de9" id="_834a90d4-d27a-480d-9295-030744bb4de9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Pablo Diego Jos&#xE9; Francisco de Paula Juan Nepomuceno Mar&#xED;a de los Remedios Cipriano de la Sant&#xED;sima Trinidad Ruiz y Picasso&#x201D; becomes &#x201C;Pablo Ruiz y Picasso&#x201D;.</p></div>
</div>
      </div>
      <div><a name="_name_profile" id="_name_profile"></a>
        <h1>8<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Name profile</h1>
        <div><a name="_general_6" id="_general_6"></a><h2>8.1 General</h2>

  <div class="figure"><a name="_c6fd5d80-b3cc-4fec-a4c6-8b06697be3d4" id="_c6fd5d80-b3cc-4fec-a4c6-8b06697be3d4"></a>
  <img src="iso-37100_files/3d55003d-71cb-4417-a47c-dc05a63296e5.png" width="400" height="210"/>
<p class="FigureTitle" align="center">Figure 4</p></div>
</div>
        <div><a name="_rendering_of_name" id="_rendering_of_name"></a><h2>8.2 Rendering of name</h2>

  
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo11;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Preference of presentation
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo11;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Contextual presentation: formal, informal, etc.
  </p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_name_concerns" id="_name_concerns"></a><h2>8.3 Name concerns</h2><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_253c6d7a-cbe5-412d-a2a7-360d8d5af40b" id="_253c6d7a-cbe5-412d-a2a7-360d8d5af40b"></a>In the rendering of a personal name, the following concerns
are important.</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo12;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    If the personal name is made to fit into a shorter string length,
which elements should remain.
  </p>

<div class="example"><a name="_66d04e8b-ff4b-49a0-93ad-0a7eecb84fa2" id="_66d04e8b-ff4b-49a0-93ad-0a7eecb84fa2"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Pablo Diego Jos&#xE9; Francisco de Paula Juan Nepomuceno Mar&#xED;a de los Remedios Cipriano de la Sant&#xED;sima Trinidad Ruiz y Picasso&#x201D; becomes &#x201C;Pablo Ruiz y Picasso&#x201D;.</p></div>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo13;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    If a personal name is made to fit into a short length,
how to render an appropriate, abbreviated name.
  </p>

<div class="example"><a name="_6ae474a0-3d2b-4122-b44f-9ede8524321c" id="_6ae474a0-3d2b-4122-b44f-9ede8524321c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Avul Pakir Jainulabdeen Abdul Kalam&#x201D;, former President of India, is
abbreviated as &#x201C;A. P. J. Abdul Kalam&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
      </div>
      <p class="MsoNormal">
