      <div class="Section3"><a name="_cultural_naming_profiles" id="_cultural_naming_profiles"></a>
        <h1 class="Annex"><b>Annex B</b><br/>(informative)<br/><br/><b>Cultural naming profiles</b></h1>
        <div><a name="_afghan" id="_afghan"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.1 Afghan</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_albanian" id="_albanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.2 Albanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_arab_muslim" id="_arab_muslim"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.3 Arab-Muslim</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bangladeshi" id="_bangladeshi"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.4 Bangladeshi</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bosnian_serbian_croats" id="_bosnian_serbian_croats"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.5 Bosnian, Serbian, Croats</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_brazilian" id="_brazilian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.6 Brazilian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bulgarian" id="_bulgarian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.7 Bulgarian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_chinese" id="_chinese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.8 Chinese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6ef1b8d9-b554-4c00-bc5c-a082d68fd6b5" id="_6ef1b8d9-b554-4c00-bc5c-a082d68fd6b5"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_0bf582be-74f5-44f2-9755-b19de2e77eb8" id="_0bf582be-74f5-44f2-9755-b19de2e77eb8"></a>Chinese personal names are names used by those from mainland China, Hong Kong,
Macau, Taiwan, and the Chinese diaspora overseas.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_10408362-8ccb-458a-95ad-4e209579cf95" id="_10408362-8ccb-458a-95ad-4e209579cf95"></a>Chinese names consist of a family name (&#x59D3;, x&#xEC;ng), followed by
a given name (&#x540D;, m&#xED;ng).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_95195c81-9eb0-4127-93b5-2327b257d164" id="_95195c81-9eb0-4127-93b5-2327b257d164"></a>Some people also utilize a &#x201C;courtesy name&#x201D; or &#x201C;style name&#x201D; (&#x5B57;, z&#xEC;),
by which they were known among those outside their
family and closest friends.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_colombian" id="_colombian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.9 Colombian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_czech" id="_czech"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.10 Czech</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_english" id="_english"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.11 English</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_42cadbbb-0ffc-4747-81c0-06f32fc22633" id="_42cadbbb-0ffc-4747-81c0-06f32fc22633"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_dc8369af-3b80-4cb8-a1dc-693ebc26fc51" id="_dc8369af-3b80-4cb8-a1dc-693ebc26fc51"></a>English names are names used in, or originating in, England. In England as
elsewhere in the English-speaking world, a complete name usually consists of a
given name, commonly referred to as a first name or Christian name, and a (most
commonly patrilineal) family name or surname, also referred to as a last name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ad0028e5-3846-4b8b-8f60-05807b8b8ed6" id="_ad0028e5-3846-4b8b-8f60-05807b8b8ed6"></a>There can be several given names, some of these being often referred to as a
second name, or middle name(s).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e83ee7e6-d4bb-4e91-8e83-00d6c8348655" id="_e83ee7e6-d4bb-4e91-8e83-00d6c8348655"></a>Double-barrelled names, compound surnames in English feature two words,
are often joined by a hyphen or hyphens.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_dbc6c82b-505e-4860-a31b-2c29f2fa4239" id="_dbc6c82b-505e-4860-a31b-2c29f2fa4239"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Henry Hepburne-Scott</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_fda673cc-46bc-4bb8-ad9f-e8739d8458c1" id="_fda673cc-46bc-4bb8-ad9f-e8739d8458c1"></a>Some families having as many as
three or four words making up their surname.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_b4b67501-6556-4c78-a0eb-4d8e45376c4d" id="_b4b67501-6556-4c78-a0eb-4d8e45376c4d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Charles Hepburn-Stuart-Forbes-Trefusis, 21st Baron Clinton.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4392cddd-c9d1-4e08-babe-661d7ef551a9" id="_4392cddd-c9d1-4e08-babe-661d7ef551a9"></a>It is not unusual for compound surnames to be composed of separate words
not linked by a hyphen.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_c6cee8ad-e9c0-4663-b3e1-a29eab10e9e7" id="_c6cee8ad-e9c0-4663-b3e1-a29eab10e9e7"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Iain Duncan Smith has a surname of &#x201C;Duncan Smith&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_french" id="_french"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.12 French</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a9a21b49-f9f1-4c4a-8583-aff67a5611c2" id="_a9a21b49-f9f1-4c4a-8583-aff67a5611c2"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c4ea471f-ac8c-4ea5-9127-f92c13112656" id="_c4ea471f-ac8c-4ea5-9127-f92c13112656"></a>French people have one, two or more given names (first names). One of them
is used in daily life.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_64ccf69a-1e47-448c-af8c-5d7171575712" id="_64ccf69a-1e47-448c-af8c-5d7171575712"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Jacques Chirac&#x201D; instead of officially of Jacques Ren&#xE9; Chirac.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_beaf27e1-44e8-4b57-834e-48757f53567c" id="_beaf27e1-44e8-4b57-834e-48757f53567c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Philippe P&#xE9;tain&#x201D; instead of officially of Henri Philippe P&#xE9;tain,
because Philippe was the given name that he used in daily
life.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_0c7277b8-483d-4fb2-86a4-a4a76dc84c85" id="_0c7277b8-483d-4fb2-86a4-a4a76dc84c85"></a>Middle names in the English sense do not exist, and middle initials are
never used for second or further given names.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_d08bff1b-a803-4ce5-9311-44ca86293bc1" id="_d08bff1b-a803-4ce5-9311-44ca86293bc1"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Claude All&#xE8;gre may be cited as &#x201C;Claude J. All&#xE8;gre&#x201D; in English-speaking scientific publications, but not in French ones.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_german" id="_german"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.13 German</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_20e17a02-3a83-4da3-bf90-d20b85db88d9" id="_20e17a02-3a83-4da3-bf90-d20b85db88d9"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_9a8bcdbc-a726-4ced-9201-73cfde02018a" id="_9a8bcdbc-a726-4ced-9201-73cfde02018a"></a>Personal names in German-speaking Europe consist of one or several given names
(Vorname, plural Vornamen) and a surname (Nachname, Familienname). The Vorname
is usually gender-specific.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2637b291-fb21-4f62-97a9-2ac6386db626" id="_2637b291-fb21-4f62-97a9-2ac6386db626"></a>Typical order of &#x201C;given name, surname&#x201D;.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_38e8fce8-a97f-4e25-8fc1-1b5b8ab8f970" id="_38e8fce8-a97f-4e25-8fc1-1b5b8ab8f970"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Otto Eduard Leopold F&#xFC;rst von Bismarck.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_5499a04c-2a18-4ccb-aef3-ae346d2b2e5e" id="_5499a04c-2a18-4ccb-aef3-ae346d2b2e5e"></a>The Vorname (in English forename) is usually given to a child by the parents
shortly after birth. It is common to give a child several Vornamen (forenames).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f2e1788a-1af2-4306-a7a2-016b4c767a02" id="_f2e1788a-1af2-4306-a7a2-016b4c767a02"></a>The Rufname (&#x201C;appellation name&#x201D; or &#x201C;call name&#x201D;) is the given name
intended for everyday use, and is often underlined on official documents.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_300672a4-fbcb-4ea8-b1ee-5071b81a70cd" id="_300672a4-fbcb-4ea8-b1ee-5071b81a70cd"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The Rufname of the mathematician Amalie Emmy Noether is &#x201C;Emmy&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_greek" id="_greek"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.14 Greek</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hindu" id="_hindu"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.15 Hindu</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hungarian" id="_hungarian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.16 Hungarian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_fbe3b018-3f06-426c-be72-96e970e93135" id="_fbe3b018-3f06-426c-be72-96e970e93135"></a>family name(s) + given name(s)</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_bebdb0a1-784d-42f8-a975-af11fad853d9" id="_bebdb0a1-784d-42f8-a975-af11fad853d9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_3f09e2c5-5518-4120-8659-40985de8a3bb" id="_3f09e2c5-5518-4120-8659-40985de8a3bb"></a>If there is more than one given name, they come in order of importance
after the family name:</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_49c46ac9-9ae6-49b1-a614-91ffb6a6ef5f" id="_49c46ac9-9ae6-49b1-a614-91ffb6a6ef5f"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos Alada&#x301;r.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_cd9485be-2ba4-4675-9205-37435338d32b" id="_cd9485be-2ba4-4675-9205-37435338d32b"></a>There can be more than one family name, which can be written fully or abbreviated
into initials:</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_b2c77090-8a9b-4e50-92ef-badd82cc4365" id="_b2c77090-8a9b-4e50-92ef-badd82cc4365"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_1de274dc-7e27-4e71-96e7-e1eb1a438939" id="_1de274dc-7e27-4e71-96e7-e1eb1a438939"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY-KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_5e71046a-f220-4043-901e-6ec87b909ee8" id="_5e71046a-f220-4043-901e-6ec87b909ee8"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  5</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>S.KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_00896960-428b-40ce-836b-58dbc53d8c3d" id="_00896960-428b-40ce-836b-58dbc53d8c3d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  6</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY K. Ja&#x301;nos</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_icelandic" id="_icelandic"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.17 Icelandic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_38df7cad-db06-4d9a-9f80-4d6da192c963" id="_38df7cad-db06-4d9a-9f80-4d6da192c963"></a>given name(s) + (patronymic or matronymic) (+ optional genonymic) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_5491656d-291b-4944-a4cf-783b6f0df232" id="_5491656d-291b-4944-a4cf-783b6f0df232"></a>Icelandic names differ from most current Western family name systems by being
patronymic or occasionally matronymic: they indicate the father (or mother) of
the child and not the historic family lineage.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_34616597-559e-4aae-b04c-4b6f77e11eed" id="_34616597-559e-4aae-b04c-4b6f77e11eed"></a>Iceland&#x2019;s traditional name system was formerly used by all Nordic countries
except partly Finland.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_59906ee7-9d05-4b46-8d6e-f66289ae4ee1" id="_59906ee7-9d05-4b46-8d6e-f66289ae4ee1"></a>The Icelandic system is not based on family names
(although some people do have family names and might use both systems).
Generally, a person&#x2019;s second name indicates the first name of their father
(patronymic) or in some cases mother (matronymic). According to Icelandic
naming tradition, second names end in -son or -d&#xF3;ttir with few exceptions.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_e3d8a96f-95c0-49bf-ab5b-175256753fb4" id="_e3d8a96f-95c0-49bf-ab5b-175256753fb4"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>J&#xF3;hanna Sigur&#xF0;ard&#xF3;ttir. &#x201C;J&#xF3;hanna, daughter of Sigur&#xF0;ur&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_bd28e2e8-dc52-4587-99b4-84c1f0f0bfb2" id="_bd28e2e8-dc52-4587-99b4-84c1f0f0bfb2"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Matronymic. Hei&#xF0;ar Helguson, &#x201C;Helguson, son of Helga&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_45ea04e4-3185-4462-8213-99bbfcae2083" id="_45ea04e4-3185-4462-8213-99bbfcae2083"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#xD6;rvar &#xDE;&#xF3;reyjarson Sm&#xE1;rason. (&#xD6;rvar, son of &#xDE;&#xF3;rey, son of Sm&#xE1;ri)</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4b9a8dac-c8f9-4bac-af1b-a40c7646ba88" id="_4b9a8dac-c8f9-4bac-af1b-a40c7646ba88"></a>Some family names do exist in Iceland, most commonly adaptations from last name
patronyms Icelanders took up when living abroad.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_e108b9a8-5205-4d64-bffe-f333a8b240a1" id="_e108b9a8-5205-4d64-bffe-f333a8b240a1"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Geir Haarde, Ei&#xF0;ur Sm&#xE1;ri Gu&#xF0;johnsen, Anita Briem.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_59cb0841-e15e-492c-9a7b-b9a892590f23" id="_59cb0841-e15e-492c-9a7b-b9a892590f23"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  5</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Nobel Prize-winning author Halld&#xF3;r Laxness, born as Halld&#xF3;r Gu&#xF0;j&#xF3;nsson.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_indian" id="_indian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.18 Indian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_indonesian" id="_indonesian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.19 Indonesian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b0c49950-f76c-497e-9759-3da5c4eb5c13" id="_b0c49950-f76c-497e-9759-3da5c4eb5c13"></a>given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_44c20260-1160-49cf-9644-046e897196d0" id="_44c20260-1160-49cf-9644-046e897196d0"></a>Indonesians generally use given names without a family name.
The majority of Indonesians do not have family names. Rather,
their given names are geographically and culturally specific.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_30adc3c6-97c7-4a23-ae52-249dcc02ecd1" id="_30adc3c6-97c7-4a23-ae52-249dcc02ecd1"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Mulyono, the original name of Joko Widodo.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_61740f6f-1a71-4782-bad6-910255448bbf" id="_61740f6f-1a71-4782-bad6-910255448bbf"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Kusno Sosrodihardjo.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_f344e5f0-4526-4abf-bf5b-f4acfe9c6b03" id="_f344e5f0-4526-4abf-bf5b-f4acfe9c6b03"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Fransiskus Xaverius Hadi Rudyatmo</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_ac008e20-bbc0-41a8-91c5-9027ca20fa4d" id="_ac008e20-bbc0-41a8-91c5-9027ca20fa4d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Sudono Salim</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a7e06704-ed07-46c0-8c79-4ac5585dfea6" id="_a7e06704-ed07-46c0-8c79-4ac5585dfea6"></a>Some given names include a family name or a patronymic.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a5834629-d438-4b4d-875b-b9e034004eff" id="_a5834629-d438-4b4d-875b-b9e034004eff"></a>Indonesian names usually fall into one of the following categories:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Mononymic names
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names without a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names with a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Polynymic names with patronymic
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f665f02c-2120-47dc-b396-a4d3ca20e7fb" id="_f665f02c-2120-47dc-b396-a4d3ca20e7fb"></a>An Indonesian name is often mis-represented in other cultures, such as:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Netherlands: the word &#x201C;Onbekend&#x201D; (&#x201C;unknown&#x201D;) is assigned as a last name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Germany: the one-word name is used as both given name and surname
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    US: &#x201C;Fnu&#x201D; (family name unknown) is assigned as the first or given name, &#x201C;Lnu&#x201D; (last name unknown) is assigned as the family, surname or last name,
or the name is duplicated as the surname.
  </p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_iranian" id="_iranian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.20 Iranian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_iraqi" id="_iraqi"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.21 Iraqi</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_italian" id="_italian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.22 Italian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_db6821d7-38d6-478b-8ea5-813f051cda2f" id="_db6821d7-38d6-478b-8ea5-813f051cda2f"></a>given name(s) + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b715c9ab-dde3-4ee3-b969-e7003115bed8" id="_b715c9ab-dde3-4ee3-b969-e7003115bed8"></a>A name in Italian consists of a given name (nome) and a surname (cognome).
Surnames are normally written after given names.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_japanese" id="_japanese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.23 Japanese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_402c1560-662a-41b7-95a7-4716424a3a3e" id="_402c1560-662a-41b7-95a7-4716424a3a3e"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a16a3581-39a9-4785-ad22-4472f51de302" id="_a16a3581-39a9-4785-ad22-4472f51de302"></a>Japanese names usually consist of a family name (surname),
followed by a given name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_17edf04b-f1f4-4abe-8ef6-af2a4df1286d" id="_17edf04b-f1f4-4abe-8ef6-af2a4df1286d"></a>Japanese names are usually written in kanji,
where characters are logographic, but each with a variety of
possible Japanese pronunciations. Therefore
often names cannot in general be spelled or
pronounced unless both the spelling and pronunciation are given.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_31be77ae-d078-4638-99e5-581487169f21" id="_31be77ae-d078-4638-99e5-581487169f21"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x7FD4; is traditionally pronounced &#x201C;Hiroto&#x201D;, but can also be pronounced as
&#x201C;Haruto&#x201D;, &#x201C;Yamato&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taiga&#x201D;, &#x201C;Sora&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taito&#x201D;, &#x201C;Daito&#x201D;, and
&#x201C;Masato&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_36ff33cc-524f-4258-8759-f4288bf853e8" id="_36ff33cc-524f-4258-8759-f4288bf853e8"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x548C; can be read as Daiwa or Yamato.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_2d1ab13e-f7a3-4933-a420-258d71b08be1" id="_2d1ab13e-f7a3-4933-a420-258d71b08be1"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x6771;&#x6D77;&#x6797; can be read as T&#x14D;kairin or Sh&#x14D;ji</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_jewish" id="_jewish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.24 Jewish</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_284f8f95-765f-4e63-b424-512560b35d25" id="_284f8f95-765f-4e63-b424-512560b35d25"></a>given name(s) + family names</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f84fa7cb-98e8-44af-8dac-410c2ca8b49d" id="_f84fa7cb-98e8-44af-8dac-410c2ca8b49d"></a>or</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_dd335759-1b64-4ae5-99d5-f6b9142e374a" id="_dd335759-1b64-4ae5-99d5-f6b9142e374a"></a>given name(s) + patronymic name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_49957e98-0ff0-4274-94ff-6994270cadc3" id="_49957e98-0ff0-4274-94ff-6994270cadc3"></a>Modern Jewish names include Hebrew patronymic names as well as family names.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_korean" id="_korean"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.25 Korean</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_702757de-934c-4c53-bc2d-3289dd24b83f" id="_702757de-934c-4c53-bc2d-3289dd24b83f"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e257e0a0-3428-4ae9-9313-904d66d78afb" id="_e257e0a0-3428-4ae9-9313-904d66d78afb"></a>A Korean name consists of a family name followed by a given name, as used by
the Korean people in both South Korea and North Korea.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2d4396dd-78e0-4d06-a262-0f1a2812df24" id="_2d4396dd-78e0-4d06-a262-0f1a2812df24"></a>In the Korean language,
ireum or seongmyeong usually refers to the family name (seong) and given name
(myeong) together.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_kurdish" id="_kurdish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.26 Kurdish</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_latvian" id="_latvian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.27 Latvian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_lithuanian" id="_lithuanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.28 Lithuanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_macedonian" id="_macedonian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.29 Macedonian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_manchu" id="_manchu"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.30 Manchu</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2200013b-357d-4640-94f3-0730144657b8" id="_2200013b-357d-4640-94f3-0730144657b8"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_8ee6b70c-1e8f-47e3-834a-6de550764c08" id="_8ee6b70c-1e8f-47e3-834a-6de550764c08"></a>Manchus are called only by their given names.
Manchu clan names (&#x201C;hala&#x201D;) are not used with their personal names,
but occasionally a family name is used.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_71a6c256-f009-414d-a806-ceab47a018b3" id="_71a6c256-f009-414d-a806-ceab47a018b3"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Tuli&#x161;en</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_16051ad3-5630-42a8-a779-85f852475ac6" id="_16051ad3-5630-42a8-a779-85f852475ac6"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Hiowan Ye (&#x7384;&#x70E8;)</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_68f3b06f-5eed-424e-b044-7423cb5db7ce" id="_68f3b06f-5eed-424e-b044-7423cb5db7ce"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Aisin-Gioro Ulhicun, linguist. The family name is &#x201C;Aisin-Gioro&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_mongolian" id="_mongolian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.31 Mongolian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_1ebc16dd-a693-4b5a-b69b-105b635aa7f1" id="_1ebc16dd-a693-4b5a-b69b-105b635aa7f1"></a>given name (+ optional clan name or family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_5484ab25-d5b9-4abb-9d33-608e10afd055" id="_5484ab25-d5b9-4abb-9d33-608e10afd055"></a>or</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_717883fe-bb0f-4814-a34e-71de2e70ce1e" id="_717883fe-bb0f-4814-a34e-71de2e70ce1e"></a>genonymic + given name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_927e1850-99bc-4c35-ae46-281c7eeb0103" id="_927e1850-99bc-4c35-ae46-281c7eeb0103"></a>Mongolian names traditionally consists of only one personal name.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_1b40bdda-88f4-42e3-8c41-8605162c5dad" id="_1b40bdda-88f4-42e3-8c41-8605162c5dad"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x41E;&#x442;&#x440;&#x44F;&#x434;&#x44B;&#x43D; &#x413;&#x4AF;&#x43D;&#x434;&#x44D;&#x433;&#x43C;&#x430;&#x430; (&#x201C;Otryadyn G&#xFC;ndegmaa&#x201D;), a Mongolian shooter, has &#x201C;Otryadyn&#x201D; as a patronym, &#x201C;G&#xFC;ndegmaa&#x201D; as given name.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_89fcca09-7043-421c-afd6-262b28db09d6" id="_89fcca09-7043-421c-afd6-262b28db09d6"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x425;&#x430;&#x43B;&#x442;&#x43C;&#x430;&#x430;&#x433;&#x438;&#x439;&#x43D; &#x411;&#x430;&#x442;&#x442;&#x443;&#x43B;&#x433;&#x430; (&#x201C;Khaltmaagiin Battulga&#x201D;), President of Mongolia.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_nigerian" id="_nigerian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.32 Nigerian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_pakistani" id="_pakistani"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.33 Pakistani</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_portuguese" id="_portuguese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.34 Portuguese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_64130cc0-3814-490f-8d8f-034a6c83e75e" id="_64130cc0-3814-490f-8d8f-034a6c83e75e"></a>given name(s) + mother&#x2019;s patronymic name(s) + father&#x2019;s patronymic name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_3f4f72a1-63f4-4f43-b5ea-b31b6f56fb9d" id="_3f4f72a1-63f4-4f43-b5ea-b31b6f56fb9d"></a>Portuguese often have two personal names, although the first may at times be used on its own.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_eddcb061-4e28-4ca0-82ac-e1ff48ba86f9" id="_eddcb061-4e28-4ca0-82ac-e1ff48ba86f9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>George Agostinho Baptista da Silva.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_0f1016b0-90c2-444b-b855-5ee1d9b7d100" id="_0f1016b0-90c2-444b-b855-5ee1d9b7d100"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Ant&#xF3;nio Caetano de Abreu Freire Egas Moniz, Nobel prize winner. He was the child of Fernando de Pina Rezende Abreu and Maria do Rosario de Almeida e Sousa.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_romanian" id="_romanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.35 Romanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_russian" id="_russian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.36 Russian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_sikh" id="_sikh"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.37 Sikh</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_sinhalese" id="_sinhalese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.38 Sinhalese</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_slavic" id="_slavic"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.39 Slavic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_efe469b4-2d33-490b-935c-1e907bc5eeed" id="_efe469b4-2d33-490b-935c-1e907bc5eeed"></a>given name + patronymic name + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2def12e9-9c0f-48c6-9127-396425d334a8" id="_2def12e9-9c0f-48c6-9127-396425d334a8"></a>Slavic naming applies to Russian, Ukrainian, Belorussian,
Bulgaria, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Serbia and Macedonia,
as well as some populations of Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_357a671a-aaf1-44b4-a888-0a98f59de474" id="_357a671a-aaf1-44b4-a888-0a98f59de474"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x418;&#x432;&#x430;&#x301;&#x43D; &#x41A;&#x43E;&#x43D;&#x441;&#x442;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x442;&#x438;&#x301;&#x43D;&#x43E;&#x432;&#x438;&#x447; &#x410;&#x439;&#x432;&#x430;&#x437;&#x43E;&#x301;&#x432;&#x441;&#x43A;&#x438;&#x439; (&#x201C;Ivan Konstantinovich Aivazovsky&#x201D;). Ivan is the given name, Konstantinovich the patronymic name, Aivazovsky the family name.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_bf214df8-5cf6-4cc7-998f-9076c55a9be5" id="_bf214df8-5cf6-4cc7-998f-9076c55a9be5"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x41E;&#x43B;&#x435;&#x43A;&#x441;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x434;&#x440; &#x406;&#x432;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x43E;&#x432;&#x438;&#x447; &#x411;&#x456;&#x43B;&#x430;&#x448;, (&#x201C;Oleksandr Ivanovych Bilash&#x201D;).  Oleksandr is the given name, Ivanovych the patronymic name, Bilash the family name.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_slovenian" id="_slovenian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.40 Slovenian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_somali" id="_somali"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.41 Somali</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_spanish" id="_spanish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.42 Spanish</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7cddb9ea-b65b-4458-a8d2-29f568e56955" id="_7cddb9ea-b65b-4458-a8d2-29f568e56955"></a>given name(s) + father&#x2019;s patronymic name(s) + mother&#x2019;s patronymic name(s)</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_07f8da9d-52d2-427d-abbf-61bcd0988fb0" id="_07f8da9d-52d2-427d-abbf-61bcd0988fb0"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Salvador de Madariaga y Rojo, Spanish diplomat, writer, historian and pacifist. Child of Dar&#xED;o Jos&#xE9; de Madariaga&#x200B; and Mar&#xED;a Ascensi&#xF3;n Rojo.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_tamil" id="_tamil"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.43 Tamil</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_thai" id="_thai"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.44 Thai</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_02a435a7-f93e-4c63-bd8d-6c149c3ca025" id="_02a435a7-f93e-4c63-bd8d-6c149c3ca025"></a>given name(s) + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a3bab2b0-bd25-4959-890b-8fe4f17cf86c" id="_a3bab2b0-bd25-4959-890b-8fe4f17cf86c"></a>Modern Thai names are composed of given names followed by the family name.
Prior to 1913, family names were not required.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_424091cb-4756-4909-a127-a572124d69b4" id="_424091cb-4756-4909-a127-a572124d69b4"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#xE0A;&#xE27;&#xE19; &#xE2B;&#xE25;&#xE35;&#xE01;&#xE20;&#xE31;&#xE22; (&#x201C;Chuan Likphai&#x201D;)</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_turkish" id="_turkish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.45 Turkish</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_ukrainian" id="_ukrainian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.46 Ukrainian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_vietnamese" id="_vietnamese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.47 Vietnamese</p>

</div>
      </div>
      <p class="MsoNormal">
        <br clear="all" style="mso-special-character:line-break;page-break-before:always"/>
      </p>
      <div>
        <h1 class="Section3">Bibliography</h1>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO9945" id="ISO9945"></a>[1]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC/IEEE 9945:2009, <i> Portable Operating System Interface (POSIX&#xAE;) Base Specifications, Issue 7</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO19788-5" id="ISO19788-5"></a>[2]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 19788-5:2012, <i> Learning, education and training</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO12234-3" id="ISO12234-3"></a>[3]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 12234-3:2016, <i> Removable memory</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO9075-1" id="ISO9075-1"></a>[4]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 9075-1:2016, <i> Database languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14813-5" id="ISO14813-5"></a>[5]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 14813-5:2010, <i> Reference model architecture(s) for the ITS sector</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14198" id="ISO14198"></a>[6]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/TS 14198:2012, <i> Ergonomic aspects of transport information and control systems</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO29003" id="ISO29003"></a>[7]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC TS 29003:2018, <i> Security techniques</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14105" id="ISO14105"></a>[8]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/TR 14105:2011, <i> Change management for successful electronic document management system (EDMS) implementation</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO20944-1" id="ISO20944-1"></a>[9]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 20944-1:2013, <i> Metadata Registries Interoperability and Bindings (MDR-IB)</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO5127" id="ISO5127"></a>[10]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 5127:2017, <i> Foundation and vocabulary</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO31320" id="ISO31320"></a>[11]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC/IEEE 31320-2:2012, <i> Modeling Languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="iso639" id="iso639"></a>[12]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 639, <i> Code for the representation of names of languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO639-3" id="ISO639-3"></a>[13]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 639-3, <i> Codes for the representation of names of languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO15961" id="ISO15961"></a>[14]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 15961:2004, <i> Radio frequency identification (RFID) for item management</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="iso15924" id="iso15924"></a>[15]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 15924:2004, <i> Codes for the representation of names of scripts</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc2046" id="rfc2046"></a>[16]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 2046, <i>Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) Part Two: Media Types</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc3986" id="rfc3986"></a>[17]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 3986, <i>Uniform Resource Identifier (URI): Generic Syntax</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc8288" id="rfc8288"></a>[18]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 8288, <i>Web Linking</i></p>
